#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161808 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "232a0173-d743-5696-a6d9-c8279514bcba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8413" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e56fbd2ced76bc02d7c9b29a81f3afab000053d11ec878040f26cef6cc5f65ce.svg)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/ba/ba1b9ecff7b4825d7eb4fa25ad706c14d04a82b8daa45e524d556c90d71700ca.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161809 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "ad5570e6-4810-5b07-a676-711f0fd069a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8414" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0cb7d70ea0921d1e2e8f2f0a9d8bc06427f29e28b8ed6ea79b5db4ba132f1e0.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_i$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161810 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "d861ebc5-8e1e-5cf0-9b82-5a64ff80cf96" :question/math-academy-id "e-8415" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb8af25c129aac1ef9268d108c073e4b895f5e154c26ddb5692fd527d4eaddc3.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/8c/8c5b88c719ee0e5495e9b0df2c9f4add97a668e74249680fbf378c7c775d92f2.svg)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/b3/b36812322e6f9128de625a8a2d4e7faf62ba98fb007224c819e2a05738cd7f98.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/16/1608541af7edcbbb06504c97b907dc2c40a97d9a7c9575d5145c67785f9eb56c.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161811 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "828e62a9-0076-5ff8-8220-4f779d48eb65" :question/math-academy-id "e-8416" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb6e660e7dd1c933fd2d5c6b6a05fbdf1fe7f5f8ed24faf15f88ea16d7d04889.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{bmatrix}-81\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/74/748df21fe8314e02f5a5253edaa39427c38600cd60099ccdfed075a28499d23e.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/08/08573d10464e356dc2eeb7a3a0a4c5f9255d0f96b14da9d63b718b766c1cc8d8.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/252867b0bccd95be918f05320ee65c7153f499b15ccc77624be18128d0f0de09.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4590f3bd-e752-5e65-a452-9b1d195950f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542828} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee1bbdc-7319-50f5-b95b-e0b9229082a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542855} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4fe57c-b619-5bf4-a435-2a7ba27bfb30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542889} {:answer/id #uuid "75c1a5b8-85c2-5eb5-b5b9-c7bab8ab41a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542951} {:answer/id #uuid "87a762ea-1f1b-52db-9e42-9df82522b93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87a762ea-1f1b-52db-9e42-9df82522b93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542998} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d2a03a5-a99e-5c9a-aa6c-8403dc252051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75ae4937-7257-5ddb-963c-2c5dab077964" :question/math-academy-id "q-140635" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ orthogonal matrix\n\nII. $Σ$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\nIII. $V$ is a diagonal matrix with only positive elements on the main diagonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $U$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be a $4\\times 4$ orthogonal matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $4\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be an orthogonal matrix, not a diagonal matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d64e4ef8-57ed-5743-bca7-960e44b7b29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369792} {:answer/id #uuid "1af36dbf-7070-5476-b26d-797d1e243e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369793} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2d111d-9382-5bea-bbb0-592e17ea1c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369794} {:answer/id #uuid "15b38ce4-1327-54ae-a86f-78a638aa2402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369795} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b1c6e0-7b4f-536e-95e8-2935f82068be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af36dbf-7070-5476-b26d-797d1e243e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369793} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39c1d66-6a3d-5e7b-8424-ecf7645db688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8011ac4-608e-5e35-aa57-26efa2ffe853" :question/math-academy-id "q-140636" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=13.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=169.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-169I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-169I=\\begin{bmatrix}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{bmatrix}-169\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-169I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{3}{5}|+|\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718aa4ab-c6a2-553c-b8b7-f653cc2600bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369799} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccf99e8-17c0-5a99-b7ab-53abb5528a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369800} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5b0b2-a831-5448-863b-c87e395627ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369801} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc10d48-fb18-5374-822d-c65d39c58de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369802} {:answer/id #uuid "a0149e98-6b1c-56c1-bfce-2d51ba4a21ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ccf99e8-17c0-5a99-b7ab-53abb5528a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369800} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe6ae0d-e8fc-5f73-8e7d-380e6efc9834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c90b8def-61bc-5437-89a5-618fac639ee8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140637" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=9.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-9I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-9I=\\begin{bmatrix}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}-9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6055e84-6449-5143-a2b3-46eff0358050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388780} {:answer/id #uuid "1f939b74-aaf7-502f-8564-0b661fd0e6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388781} {:answer/id #uuid "02624a8f-1b6a-5107-a65b-691283905860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388782} {:answer/id #uuid "a8527eb6-3fa6-5558-a401-122a4ccf2932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388783} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ff1e-f85f-5965-ad45-394bcfc443a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02624a8f-1b6a-5107-a65b-691283905860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388782} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7af0c55-c0c0-57b2-8764-cee03bfd3605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "869bbc7e-c91d-54b2-899a-a9769b330e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-140643" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3ea076-4d0b-50b5-af4f-ca0b0399c71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495613} {:answer/id #uuid "3143f9ac-e07a-56d2-986f-b0a7f64340bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495622} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3656be-5473-5356-8bab-68c6041eb7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495651} {:answer/id #uuid "5afcdc04-e77f-5dcd-84c4-8d2f63b67107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495716} {:answer/id #uuid "90a84480-b5fe-5e99-a5ca-05d902ef19d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3143f9ac-e07a-56d2-986f-b0a7f64340bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495622} :answer-field/id #uuid "20030bd4-6e8a-5215-b9ee-0ffa2a972515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9582267b-adc4-536b-9335-9619155f3b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-140646" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=64,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-64I & =\\begin{bmatrix}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{bmatrix}-64\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-27 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-64I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =\\begin{bmatrix}37 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 52\\end{bmatrix}-25\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 27\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)x=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{16}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the Third Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f091165-8723-50ab-b788-42eed3a5af97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161277} {:answer/id #uuid "c59a886f-a8e9-5c1a-83c5-0a1690532273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161278} {:answer/id #uuid "c75b2b50-3ec3-5808-b5ec-a1a4851138b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161279} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b5d11f-d085-5957-ab58-25b88f1e8f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161280} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8dbebe-20bc-5b8e-a384-b6ffc2c89a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c59a886f-a8e9-5c1a-83c5-0a1690532273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161278} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebbd260-66b0-55ac-b9f9-24d9e4e96e0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd780784-8f83-5b82-bd42-f51c45f9bf15" :question/math-academy-id "q-95727" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-4I=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "761c0855-151d-5c28-a33c-0a024467af2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385861} {:answer/id #uuid "54dd6618-49c1-5345-aa31-f35e307bf495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385862} {:answer/id #uuid "f649cc86-0ff8-5df8-bae6-aa464f130472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385863} {:answer/id #uuid "e5504555-3d0f-535c-b1c6-d95fb2d03bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385864} {:answer/id #uuid "53e04011-d369-5c0e-8319-75cfcd898bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "761c0855-151d-5c28-a33c-0a024467af2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385861} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ba4b1a-699c-5984-810a-3a206a0122be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd1fb855-b7cc-5891-8c39-1b5365c25012" :question/math-academy-id "q-95905" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332ee583-e727-5bba-8acd-922d23695b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395088} {:answer/id #uuid "a10add95-adc6-5235-9f97-1b43eca22a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395089} {:answer/id #uuid "cd26ab61-b779-5a11-875e-496dff8002aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395090} {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395091} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4b6724-9b4a-518a-bc3a-5a3c01258664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193f3747-9a2d-5cbb-a6bc-941942f42e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395091} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d28a695-b25d-5276-8883-5cca1cebedbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5db18361-738b-5a9a-b3b0-1db51bcde1bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95911" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-100I & =\\begin{bmatrix}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{bmatrix}-100\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 30 \\\\ 30 & -60\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =\\begin{bmatrix}85 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{bmatrix}-25\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}60 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 10\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e43e58b-e171-5bde-8e66-fa1047e3a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404838} {:answer/id #uuid "ad42d289-8707-56c1-bf83-45db2fe36ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} {:answer/id #uuid "999d413b-60b1-5bd7-abe7-54bb8a4e91b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404840} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe630aa-18c2-5c17-a5c7-5d9c6cf67fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404841} {:answer/id #uuid "7758da6c-c911-54cb-96cc-7117ea4fabca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad42d289-8707-56c1-bf83-45db2fe36ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404839} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca27652a-1905-5111-863a-956b813d6f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "069d96cb-6a82-58f7-9653-ba67b55165b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95912" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}-16\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285a9780-e86c-548a-adb5-cbb884e8ca6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495598} {:answer/id #uuid "2d03c4f6-f195-5e6b-866b-9bb346f9fc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495611} {:answer/id #uuid "319bb27d-e8dd-5212-8869-65e8ae228b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495624} {:answer/id #uuid "ea634426-335f-5311-b804-0a4c4997b3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496027} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ec482c-c5db-502f-b6f8-1aae7843448d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "319bb27d-e8dd-5212-8869-65e8ae228b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495624} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36a171f-653b-5a9e-b0c3-ea8ba71c938e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0773915-2806-5187-a512-1daa0afdcd4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96118" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $U$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\nII. $U$ is an orthogonal matrix\n\nIII. $Σ$ is an orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, $U$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the matrix $U$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $Σ$ must be diagonal but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2e6c66-9a79-55e3-b1e0-94a4d29ebfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454238} {:answer/id #uuid "7a02bb30-1669-593f-af54-15cf2d69a411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a02bb30-1669-593f-af54-15cf2d69a411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454239} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b94f0d-44e4-5954-a592-c0a67cf046d3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454869} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7d9711-e8da-5402-9353-d7a54c6fdd1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454240} {:answer/id #uuid "258167a2-8c9b-5c15-b4d3-d269611a65f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca7d9711-e8da-5402-9353-d7a54c6fdd1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454240} :answer-field/id #uuid "9075c6d7-7962-5c2f-853b-b0a4037eac22" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec35b84-9702-578b-bcaa-46c83d81452c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454242} {:answer/id #uuid "b986e593-ff87-5dce-80c3-5466a385fd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b986e593-ff87-5dce-80c3-5466a385fd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454243} :answer-field/id #uuid "39546f24-c90a-5e38-bfbe-6ba14074b842" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3302b4c-3bdd-5b04-9e3e-aac900425c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96120" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Σ$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix\n- **II.** $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix with only positive elements on the main diagonal\n- **III.** $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $5\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $Σ$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be diagonal with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal. The main diagonal may contain zeroes.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4abf63e6-f726-5e62-877b-e55ec936e71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495683} {:answer/id #uuid "94c97f0b-950e-53ee-8528-58cf113935e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495839} {:answer/id #uuid "9aea7879-dd37-548f-a388-79a0c1cba990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495855} {:answer/id #uuid "cecc1d54-52d9-5a5f-8a1d-bfaee1aac033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495960} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c11591-dfe6-5782-9a57-f4732952a8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4abf63e6-f726-5e62-877b-e55ec936e71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495683} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e0de73-fab0-5dbe-bb6a-4caec9eea734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6cc8412-6a80-579d-ba3c-43e9e90968b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96232" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55eda9fc-807f-5348-8813-d854a5b33209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418103} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb3ccbb-6f9a-5137-a3b4-d0a87f0e7267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418109} {:answer/id #uuid "781d2783-b360-5c7a-a6be-21fa965bb5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{b